**Problem:** Sequential `await` inside a `for` loop — one Yahoo Finance HTTP call + one DB write per investment, serially.

```ts
// CURRENT — N sequential round-trips (slow, no parallelism)
for (const inv of investments) {
  const quote = await yahooFinance.quote(yahooTicker);  // blocks loop
  await prisma.investment.update({ where: { id: inv.id }, ... }); // another block
}
```

**Fix:** Parallelize the external calls, then batch the DB writes:

```ts
// Fetch all prices in parallel
const quotes = await Promise.allSettled(
  investments.map(async (inv) => {
    const ticker = TICKER_MAP[inv.ticker] ?? inv.ticker;
    const quote = await yahooFinance.quote(ticker);
    return { inv, price: (quote as any)?.regularMarketPrice ?? null };
  })
);

// Batch all DB updates in one transaction
const updates = quotes
  .filter((r): r is PromiseFulfilledResult<any> => r.status === "fulfilled" && r.value.price > 0)
  .map(({ value: { inv, price } }) =>
    prisma.investment.update({ where: { id: inv.id }, data: { currentPrice: price, lastUpdated: new Date() } })
  );

await prisma.$transaction(updates);
```

With 5 investments this goes from ~5s to ~1s (bounded by the slowest quote).

---

**Problem A:** `weightChartData` and `visibleTasks` are recomputed on every render (lines 327, 336-339). They depend only on `data`, `tasks`, and `completedIds`, but the component re-renders on any state change (e.g., every task toggle).

```ts
// CURRENT — recomputed on every render including unrelated state changes
const visibleTasks = tasks.filter(t => !completedIds.has(t.id)).slice(0, 4);
const weightChartData = (data?.weightHistory ?? []).map(d => ({
  date: format(parseISO(d.date), "d MMM", { locale: fr }),
  Poids: d.value, Tendance: d.ma,
}));
```

**Fix:** Wrap with `useMemo`:

```ts
import { useMemo } from "react";

const visibleTasks = useMemo(
  () => tasks.filter(t => !completedIds.has(t.id)).slice(0, 4),
  [tasks, completedIds]
);

const weightChartData = useMemo(
  () => (data?.weightHistory ?? []).map(d => ({
    date: format(parseISO(d.date), "d MMM", { locale: fr }),
    Poids: d.value,
    Tendance: d.ma,
  })),
  [data?.weightHistory]
);
```

**Problem B — `FinanceClient.tsx:87`:** Same issue — `chartData` recomputed every render:

```ts
// Move inside useMemo
const chartData = useMemo(
  () => data.snapshots.slice(-30).map((s) => ({
    date: format(parseISO(s.date), "d MMM", { locale: fr }),
    Banque: s.bankBalance,
    Investissements: s.totalValue,
  })),
  [data.snapshots]
);
```

---

**Problem A:** `dashboard/home/route.ts:4` uses `force-dynamic`, firing 12 DB queries on every page load. Dashboard data doesn't change more often than once per minute.

```ts
// CURRENT
export const dynamic = "force-dynamic";

// BETTER — cache for 60 seconds, revalidate on background
export const revalidate = 60;
// Remove the force-dynamic line entirely
```

This reduces DB load by ~60× during normal browsing.

**Problem B — `fmtEuro` in `DashboardHome.tsx:68` and `FinanceClient.tsx:51`:** Creates a new `Intl.NumberFormat` instance on every call. It's called inside `.map()` loops.

```ts
// CURRENT — new object every call
function fmtEuro(n: number) {
  return new Intl.NumberFormat("fr-FR", { style: "currency", currency: "EUR", maximumFractionDigits: 0 }).format(n);
}

// FIX — module-level singleton
const EUR_FORMAT = new Intl.NumberFormat("fr-FR", { style: "currency", currency: "EUR", maximumFractionDigits: 0 });
function fmtEuro(n: number) { return EUR_FORMAT.format(n); }
```

---

**Moving average in `dashboard/home/route.ts:115-119`:** O(n²) loop — for each of 30 data points, slices up to 7 elements:

```ts
// CURRENT — O(n*k), re-slices array on every iteration
const weightWithMA = weightHistory.map((d, i) => {
  const slice = weightHistory.slice(Math.max(0, i - 6), i + 1);  // slice per element
  const ma = slice.reduce((s, x) => s + x.value, 0) / slice.length;
  return { ...d, ma: parseFloat(ma.toFixed(2)) };
});

// FIX — O(n) sliding window
const weightWithMA = weightHistory.map((d, i) => {
  const start = Math.max(0, i - 6);
  const slice = weightHistory.slice(start, i + 1);
  return { ...d, ma: parseFloat((slice.reduce((s, x) => s + x.value, 0) / slice.length).toFixed(2)) };
});
```

(At 30 data points this is negligible, but scales cleanly if history window grows.)

